-In 抽象, 抽 supplies チュウ and 象 supplies ショウ, with no sound change; in 抽出, 出 supplies シュツ, and in 抽選, 選 supplies セン. The trailing dash in ひき- marks a prefix use, one that comes before another element; it is not pronounced. In 抽斗, 抽 writes ひき and 斗 writes だし; Daijisen lists 抽斗 ひきだし as a hard-to-read word. JMdict also lists 抽出し, where 抽 writes ひき, 出 writes だ and し is okurigana, the kana ending that follows the kanji, as a search-only spelling. In the rarely used spelling 抽き出し, 抽 writes only ひ and き is okurigana; KANJIDIC2 lists ひき- for 抽 but no verb reading ひ.く.
+In 抽象, 抽 supplies チュウ and 象 supplies ショウ, with no sound change; in 抽出, 出 supplies シュツ, and in 抽選, 選 supplies セン. The trailing dash in ひき- marks a prefix use, one that comes before another element; it is not pronounced. In 抽出し, 抽 writes ひき, 出 writes だ and し is okurigana, the kana ending that follows the kanji; the same two kanji are read ちゅうしゅつ in 抽出. In the rarely used spelling 抽き出し, 抽 writes only ひ and き is okurigana; KANJIDIC2 lists ひき- for 抽 but no verb reading ひ.く. Daijisen lists the rarely used spelling 抽斗 ひきだし as a hard-to-read word; this is a reading of the whole word, not a kanji-by-kanji reading, and KANJIDIC2 gives 斗 no reading だし.
 
 The official Joyo table, the list of standard-use readings, shows チュウ, with 抽出 and 抽象 as examples. ひき- is not in the table. Practice selects チュウ, the reading with a linked word article. Being outside the practice set is not proof that a reading is invalid.
 
